hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  it is groundwork of the metaphysics of  morals Immanuel Kant at a certain point  is going to give us a rather schematic  treatment of what he is calling these  three modes of presenting the principle  of morality the the principle that we've  essentially been looking for is the the  core the touchstone for ethics or for  moral theory itself and he says these  three different ways of presenting it  are indeed interconnected each of them  it it involves or includes we might even  say in a certain respect implies the  other two they lead to each other that  in fact is what he's carrying out in his  analysis in the groundwork and another  thing that he says is although they are  different they really are subjectively  rather than objectively different one  way in which it's framed is that these  are the difference is subjectively  rather than objectively practical what  is the the idea there it's to bring an  idea of reason something that that human  rationality is able to understand closer  to what Kant calls intuition closer to  our grasping it and he says we do this  by way of a certain analogy now he  doesn't explain exactly what he means by  analogy there and that's something very  ripe for for discussion but what we want  to focus on are these three different  ways of looking at the same basic thing  and you notice that these are going to  correspond to the three formulations or  the three ways of understanding the  categorical imperative discussed here in  the groundwork and he tells us that we  have a for every Maxim for every every  rule that we give ourselves by which we  would determine our  own will every every rule for deciding  you might say by which we explain it or  justify it to ourselves or could it  count to another person for what it is  that we're doing and why we're doing it  it has a form it has a matter and then  if you have something that is a little  bit more difficult to translate a  complete determination is a great way of  translating it whole standing abashed  among in in German there's other there's  other ways in which this does get  translated for example complete  characterization there's there's a  variety of ways but still means these  things are connected with each other and  they are made you know in some respect  they're made concrete they're made  relational the you know determination I  think is the best word for capturing it  now what does that mean so if we take  any sort of Maxim I you know when a  situation is like this then I will  behave in this way whenever I have this  motive and I'm in this sort of case I  will do this  what Conte is saying is that it has a  form and the form is its universality  and this is the universality of what  this is that that first formulation of  the categorical imperative I'll act  always on the maxim notice Maxim that  you can at the same time will to be a  universal law of nature now it may not  actually be a universal law of nature  but you should be able to will that that  the world could actually look like what  it would be if everybody behaved the  same way as you and if everybody had to  behave the same way as you why is it the  form well because that tells us how  indeed we want to be acting what is what  is the thing that connects them all  together my actions and other people's  actions I'm not actually saying  everybody will act like me but I will in  such a way that that form could be sort  of you transposed onto everything else  then we have a matter and that matter is  the end and  and is an end in itself here we have the  second formulation of the categorical  imperative right act always in such a  way that you you know treat humanity  never is a mere means but always as an  end in itself treat humanity in yourself  in another people right so that is is a  central to it as well and now we're  starting to get a little bit more  concrete this is why sometimes people  find it easier to make sense out of that  formulation the second formulation of  the categorical imperative then the  first formulation it all depends on how  you're looking at it then we get to the  really interesting one here we don't  have a thing that corresponds to a  universality or end right  the complete determination as Kant is  going to tell us complete determination  of all Maxim's by means of a formula and  the formula is that all Maxim's ought to  by their own legislation by their own  way in which they're they're setting  things down normatively ought to  harmonize with a possible kingdom of  ends as with a kingdom of nature so what  does that mean well we have another  lecture specifically just on that third  formulation but the idea is that now  this goes beyond simply saying well  whatever I happen to do it should be a  universal law of nature if they can't  meet that test and I shouldn't do that  sort of thing right or I have if I want  to claim that what I'm doing actually is  in accordance with duty it has to be  universally applicable to everybody or  but I'm treating everybody else as as an  end or not treating myself as a mere  means either here we're bringing the two  together and there's a kind of you might  even say synthesis taking place here now  Kant will go on and say by the way this  connects up with my way of  looking at categories of quantity you  know that he's discussed in other other  works and he's going to be talking about  in the Katif that this is you know sort  of leading towards he says here we go  there's a progress here in the order of  the categories of something right of the  form of the will right the unity of the  form of the will no longer just the  maximum the unity einheit choosing  something that is coherent that can be a  unity that can be applied in every  single case the same way plurality  plurality of the matter of the will what  do we tell I is it plurality feel height  in German here because we're already  look looking at ends and means right we  have a plurality but we can also think  about self and other right humanity in  myself humanity in the other person  there there a variety of ways in which I  could treat somebody myself or the other  as a means rather than as an ends then  we have when can't hear calls totality  and actually he says all height odor  totally Tet in the German text right we  could call it the epithet is just a you  know a cognate right of totality all  height you know everything the entirety  right and that is a little bit more  complex right he says here we go the  totality of the system of these so  totality encompasses these two and what  we see is that the thereby the third  formulation of the categorical  imperative or the third mode of  presentation  the principle of morality is in a  certain way the one that encompasses the  other two and brings them together this  is very important because remember the  groundwork for the metaphysics of morals  is not by itself constant higher ethics  but it's supposed to be a preparation a  groundwork right for the actual  metaphysics of morals which has two  parts one is the you know doctrine of  virtue and the other is the doctrine of  justice which has to do with the  totality of actual human societies and  how legislation want to take place in  those so what we have here is sort of an  interesting schematic way of presenting  the three modes of presentation of the  same one thing the principle of morality  the three different formulations the  three different ways of looking at it as  Kant said from the beginning as they  want to reiterate each of these does  involve the other this is really about  you know finding the best way of  presenting our understanding of duty and  of autonomy to particular people so some  people may pick up on one better than  they pick up on another at least for a  certain time generally I find most  people pick up on the second one better  than the first let alone the third but  these these provide us with ways to get  at what morality is according to Kant
 

